'Imagine each pupil placing one counter above the number of library books they borrowed. As counters gather into stacks, some numbers may be more common than others. Before naming a most common value, look at the whole arrangement. What story might those stacks tell about the class?',
'Our goal is to describe the mode and the whole distribution in context. We want to say what the most common value means, but also notice pupils whose values are different. A single summary is a useful clue, rather than the entire investigation.',
'Remember that frequency means how often a value occurs. In a column graph, the horizontal labels name the book counts and the vertical scale counts pupils. Read both labels before giving an answer. Two books and three pupils describe different parts of the same display.',
'Build the first display one pupil at a time. Place one counter at zero books, one at one book, then three counters at two books. Finally place one at three and one at four. We can replace each stack by a column without changing its frequency. Now the concrete counters and the graph show the same data.',
'Before hearing the answer, compare the column heights. Which book-count value belongs to the tallest column? Think about how you would finish this sentence: the most common number of books is something, because something pupils borrowed that many. Keep the value and its frequency separate.',
'Count the three pupils at two books, then trace down to the label two. That label is the mode. One pupil borrowed zero, and another borrowed four, so it would be wrong to say everyone borrowed two. The distribution is balanced around two, with matching frequencies on the two sides.',
