'Class A has pupils on both sides of two books. Class B has no pupils at zero or one, and all its observations are two, three or four. Both classes have the same tallest value at two books. Their shared mode does not make the two sets of observations identical. Use the empty positions and the other column heights as evidence.',
'Imagine one extra pupil reporting four books. The highest numerical value has changed in frequency, but that alone does not make four the mode. Compare all frequencies again. A mode is chosen by how often a value occurs, rather than by how large the value is.',
'Try a tied distribution. If one book and two books each have five pupils, and every other frequency is smaller, both are modes. If all the displayed possible values have the same frequency, no single value stands out as a useful mode. Report what the graph shows instead of forcing a single answer.',
